  "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: six pictures of the same typical 1970s Finnish suburban concrete apartment building, the unrenovated building (Image 1, made anonymous by image editing) and five visualisations of wooden facade renovation with additional wooden floors: Image 2 style with minimal solution (one extra floor), Image 3 modern look (asymmetrical, one and a half floors), Image 4 inspiration from suburban architecture (two floors, outside elevators), Image 5 implementation of the gable roof and Image 6 wood in main parts (natural wood cladding). Participants first answer the paper's attitude questions, How do you think about wooden facade renovation? and about wooden additional floor construction (positively, partially positively, neutral, partially negatively, negatively, I do not know), how both affect the attractiveness of residential areas, and four statements (completely agree to completely disagree, I do not know). They then see the six pictures in the paper's order, Image 1 first, and grade each on the Finnish school grading scale from 4 (I don't like the look of the building and I have a negative attitude like this building) to 10 (appearance of the building is beautiful and I welcome this building), with an optional reason. Gender, age group, education and housing are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Karjalainen, Ilgın, Metsäranta & Norvasuo (2021), Residents' Attitudes towards Wooden Facade Renovation and Additional Floor Construction in Finland, International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 18(23): 12316, with the pictures cropped from its Figure 2 and the question texts and answer options from its Table 1 and Figures 3-5 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: image1-image6. Analysis as in the paper: answer shares by gender, age group, education and housing for the attitude questions, and mean grades and grade distributions per picture; the paper (243 residents aged 18-70 of the Pukinmäki-Savela area in Helsinki, postal and online survey) found 91% positive or partially positive about wooden facade renovation, the unrenovated building graded 5.44 against 7.65 on average for the proposals, which differed only slightly (Image 5 most popular, then 2, 3, 4 and 6). Differences from the original study: (1) parts D (preferred kinds of additional construction) and E (apartment owners' reaction to a housing association decision) are not included; (2) the introduction text, the stem of the grade question (the paper prints only the scale and its two end labels), the reason question and the background questions are ours (the paper reports gender, age groups, education and housing but does not print the questions; their answer groups follow its Figure 3, and the 'Other' options are ours); (3) the paper introduced the topic with a series of pictures on the economic benefits of additional floor construction, which it does not print, so a short text is used instead; (4) the paper used Finnish paper and online forms for residents of one Helsinki area; here the English wording of its figures is used and anyone can take part.",
